time, none of which requires the artist

273
00:10:11,440 --> 00:10:15,760
to do anything at all. Pay attention to

274
00:10:13,680 --> 00:10:17,600
this next detail because it explains

275
00:10:15,760 --> 00:10:19,839
everything about why the modern label

276
00:10:17,600 --> 00:10:21,680
looks the way it does. Publishing and

277
00:10:19,839 --> 00:10:24,079
sync are what the label earns from the

278
00:10:21,680 --> 00:10:26,320
song. What comes next is what the label

279
00:10:24,079 --> 00:10:28,240
earns from the artist. For most of the

280
00:10:26,320 --> 00:10:30,240
history of the music industry, a record

281
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:32,399
deal was exactly what it sounds like.

282
00:10:30,240 --> 00:10:34,320
The label funded the record, the label

283
00:10:32,399 --> 00:10:36,399
owned the record, and the label made

284
00:10:34,320 --> 00:10:38,720
money when the record sold. Everything

285
00:10:36,399 --> 00:10:40,560
else the artist did, their touring,

286
00:10:38,720 --> 00:10:43,279
their merchandise, their endorsement

287
00:10:40,560 --> 00:10:45,760
deals, that was the artist business. The

288
00:10:43,279 --> 00:10:47,839
label didn't touch it. That changed. And

289
00:10:45,760 --> 00:10:50,320
the change has a name. It's called the

290
00:10:47,839 --> 00:10:52,640
360 deal, and it is now the standard

291
00:10:50,320 --> 00:10:55,680
contract at every major label. Here's

292
00:10:52,640 --> 00:10:58,079
how a 360 deal works. The label still

293
00:10:55,680 --> 00:11:01,440
does everything it always did. It pays

294
00:10:58,079 --> 00:11:03,600
for recording, production, marketing,

295
00:11:01,440 --> 00:11:05,760
distribution, but instead of only taking

296
00:11:03,600 --> 00:11:07,600
a cut of the recorded music, the label

297
00:11:05,760 --> 00:11:09,839
now takes a percentage of essentially

298
00:11:07,600 --> 00:11:12,079
everything the artist earns. Touring

299
00:11:09,839 --> 00:11:14,959
revenue, the label gets a piece.

300
00:11:12,079 --> 00:11:16,640
Typically somewhere between 10 and 50%

301
00:11:14,959 --> 00:11:18,720
depending on how much leverage the

302
00:11:16,640 --> 00:11:20,880
artist has at the negotiating table.

303
00:11:18,720 --> 00:11:24,160
Merchandise sales, the label gets a

304
00:11:20,880 --> 00:11:26,399
piece, usually 10 to 25%. Brand

305
00:11:24,160 --> 00:11:28,959
partnerships and endorsement deals, the

306
00:11:26,399 --> 00:11:31,600
label gets a piece. If a shoe company

307
00:11:28,959 --> 00:11:33,600
pays an artist a million to wear their

308
00:11:31,600 --> 00:11:36,079
sneakers, the label takes its

309
00:11:33,600 --> 00:11:37,839
contractual share of that check, too. Go

310
00:11:36,079 --> 00:11:40,160
back to the landlord analogy for a

311
00:11:37,839 --> 00:11:41,680
second. Imagine your landlord not only

312
00:11:40,160 --> 00:11:44,079
collects rent on the building you live

313
00:11:41,680 --> 00:11:45,760
in, but also takes a percentage every

314
00:11:44,079 --> 00:11:47,920
time you sell lemonade on the front

315
00:11:45,760 --> 00:11:50,079
porch. Every time someone pays you to

316
00:11:47,920 --> 00:11:52,079
wear a t-shirt with their logo on it,

317
00:11:50,079 --> 00:11:55,279
and every time a company hires you to

318
00:11:52,079 --> 00:11:57,279
show up at an event, that's a 360 deal.

319
00:11:55,279 --> 00:11:59,360
The label is making money off everything

320
00:11:57,279 --> 00:12:01,760
the artist does, not just their

321
00:11:59,360 --> 00:12:04,079
recordings. Now, layer on top of that

322
00:12:01,760 --> 00:12:05,920
the way the money actually flows. When a

323
00:12:04,079 --> 00:12:08,480
label signs an artist, it gives them

324
00:12:05,920 --> 00:12:10,639
what's called an advance. This is the

325
00:12:08,480 --> 00:12:12,959
cash the artist gets upfront. The

326
00:12:10,639 --> 00:12:15,279
recording budget, the living expenses,

327
00:12:12,959 --> 00:12:17,040
the marketing spend, sometimes hundreds

328
00:12:15,279 --> 00:12:19,279
of thousands or even millions of

329
00:12:17,040 --> 00:12:21,360
dollars. And from the outside, this

330
00:12:19,279 --> 00:12:23,440
looks like a paycheck. It looks like the

331
00:12:21,360 --> 00:12:25,680
label is investing in the artist and

332
00:12:23,440 --> 00:12:28,240
paying them for their talent. It is not

333
00:12:25,680 --> 00:12:30,480
a paycheck. It is a loan. The label

334
00:12:28,240 --> 00:12:33,200
calls it an advance because it is money

335
00:12:30,480 --> 00:12:35,200
advanced against future royalties. From

336
00:12:33,200 --> 00:12:37,200
the moment that advance is paid, every

337
00:12:35,200 --> 00:12:39,680
dollar the artist earns in royalties

338
00:12:37,200 --> 00:12:42,240
goes back to the label first to pay off

339
00:12:39,680 --> 00:12:44,560
the advance. The artist doesn't see a

340
00:12:42,240 --> 00:12:46,720
single royalty check until the label has

341
00:12:44,560 --> 00:12:49,279
recovered every penny it spent. That

342
00:12:46,720 --> 00:12:51,920
process is called recoupment. And in a

343
00:12:49,279 --> 00:12:53,519
lot of deals, it never happens. The

344
00:12:51,920 --> 00:12:55,440
artist never earns enough to pay back

345
00:12:53,519 --> 00:12:57,839
the advance, which means the artist

346
00:12:55,440 --> 00:12:59,680
never sees royalties at all. The label,

347
00:12:57,839 --> 00:13:01,760
meanwhile, still owns the master

348
00:12:59,680 --> 00:13:03,920
recordings from that deal forever. And

349
00:13:01,760 --> 00:13:05,920
it gets more complicated than that. Many

350
00:13:03,920 --> 00:13:07,519
label contracts include something called

351
00:13:05,920 --> 00:13:09,360
crosscolateralization,

352
00:13:07,519 --> 00:13:11,519
which means the label can combine the

353
00:13:09,360 --> 00:13:14,079
financial performance of multiple albums

354
00:13:11,519 --> 00:13:16,240
under the same contract. So if an

355
00:13:14,079 --> 00:13:18,480
artist's second album is a hit, the

356
00:13:16,240 --> 00:13:20,560
royalties from that hit can be used to

357
00:13:18,480 --> 00:13:22,959
pay off the unrecouped balance from the

358
00:13:20,560 --> 00:13:25,279
first album before the artist sees any

359
00:13:22,959 --> 00:13:27,519
money from either one. The label pulls

360
00:13:25,279 --> 00:13:30,399
the wins and the losses together, and

361
00:13:27,519 --> 00:13:32,240
the losses always get paid first. If

362
00:13:30,399 --> 00:13:35,200
you're wondering how that's even legal,

363
00:13:32,240 --> 00:13:37,600
so is everyone else. But it is legal and

364
00:13:35,200 --> 00:13:39,600
it is industry standard. And it is the

365
00:13:37,600 --> 00:13:42,079
reason why so many artists you've heard

366
00:13:39,600 --> 00:13:44,399
of, artists whose songs you've streamed

367
00:13:42,079 --> 00:13:46,480
millions of times, have talked publicly

368
00:13:44,399 --> 00:13:49,040
about being broke while their labels are

369
00:13:46,480 --> 00:13:51,360
reporting record profits. But forget for

370
00:13:49,040 --> 00:13:53,040
a second whether this system is fair.

371
00:13:51,360 --> 00:13:54,959
Look at what happens when you zoom out

372
00:13:53,040 --> 00:13:57,199
and see these companies for what they've

373
00:13:54,959 --> 00:13:59,760
actually become. Because this isn't just

374
00:13:57,199 --> 00:14:01,279
about one artist or one deal anymore.

375
00:13:59,760 --> 00:14:02,639
When you pull back and look at what

376
00:14:01,279 --> 00:14:04,720
these companies have become at the

377
00:14:02,639 --> 00:14:06,639
corporate level, the picture changes

378
00:14:04,720 --> 00:14:09,120
completely. While we've been looking at

379
00:14:06,639 --> 00:14:10,800
how one artist's deal works, something

380
00:14:09,120 --> 00:14:13,120
much bigger has been happening at the

381
00:14:10,800 --> 00:14:15,519
corporate level. And it changes what you

382
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:17,120
think a music label actually is. When

383
00:14:15,519 --> 00:14:18,959
Universal Music Group signs a new

384
00:14:17,120 --> 00:14:20,959
artist, they are not betting on a hit

385
00:14:18,959 --> 00:14:23,120
single. They are not gambling on whether

386
00:14:20,959 --> 00:14:25,440
one song goes viral on TikTok or whether

387
00:14:23,120 --> 00:14:27,600
one album debuts at number one. What

388
00:14:25,440 --> 00:14:29,839
they are doing in financial terms is

389
00:14:27,600 --> 00:14:32,399
acquiring a long-term incomeroucing

390
00:14:29,839 --> 00:14:34,720
asset. The advance they pay is the

391
00:14:32,399 --> 00:14:37,279
purchase price. The recordings they own

392
00:14:34,720 --> 00:14:39,760
are the asset. And the revenue that

393
00:14:37,279 --> 00:14:42,480
asset generates over the next 20, 30, 50

394
00:14:39,760 --> 00:14:44,399
years is the return on investment. No

395
00:14:42,480 --> 00:14:46,560
creative company thinks that way, but a

396
00:14:44,399 --> 00:14:48,240
holding company does. And that is

397
00:14:46,560 --> 00:14:50,560
exactly what the major labels have

398
00:14:48,240 --> 00:14:52,720
become. They are intellectual property

399
00:14:50,560 --> 00:14:55,199
holding companies that use artists as

400
00:14:52,720 --> 00:14:57,040
their primary acquisition vehicles. Look

401
00:14:55,199 --> 00:14:59,440
at the catalog deals again through this

402
00:14:57,040 --> 00:15:01,279
lens. Bob Dylan's combined catalog sold

403
00:14:59,440 --> 00:15:03,680
for more than half a billion dollars.

404
00:15:01,279 --> 00:15:06,000
Springsteen went for 500 million, but

405
00:15:03,680 --> 00:15:07,760
those weren't sentimental purchases.

406
00:15:06,000 --> 00:15:09,360
Universal and Sony didn't buy those

407
00:15:07,760 --> 00:15:11,440
cataloges because they love classic

408
00:15:09,360 --> 00:15:13,120
rock. They bought them because a catalog

409
00:15:11,440 --> 00:15:16,000
that has been generating predictable

410
00:15:13,120 --> 00:15:18,240
royalty income for 30 or 40 years is an

411
00:15:16,000 --> 00:15:20,560
asset you can model. You can forecast

412
00:15:18,240 --> 00:15:22,639
its revenue. You can project its growth

413
00:15:20,560 --> 00:15:24,399
rate based on streaming trends. You can

414
00:15:22,639 --> 00:15:26,240
calculate its yield the same way you'd

415
00:15:24,399 --> 00:15:28,240
calculate the yield on a rental property

416
00:15:26,240 --> 00:15:30,959
or a bond. What the label is really

417
00:15:28,240 --> 00:15:33,360
paying for is the catalog. The artist's

418
00:15:30,959 --> 00:15:35,040
career is the process that builds it.

419
00:15:33,360 --> 00:15:37,120
This framing explains something that

420
00:15:35,040 --> 00:15:39,680
confuses a lot of people about the music

421
00:15:37,120 --> 00:15:41,760
industry. Why do labels keep signing

422
00:15:39,680 --> 00:15:44,240
artists who might never be profitable on

423
00:15:41,760 --> 00:15:46,480
their own? Why pour money into a debut

424
00:15:44,240 --> 00:15:48,160
album that barely breaks even? because

425
00:15:46,480 --> 00:15:50,160
the label isn't trying to make money on

426
00:15:48,160 --> 00:15:52,399
that album. The label is trying to

427
00:15:50,160 --> 00:15:54,480
acquire the recording. If the artist

428
00:15:52,399 --> 00:15:56,720
breaks through, the catalog becomes

429
00:15:54,480 --> 00:15:58,720
enormously valuable. If the artist

430
00:15:56,720 --> 00:16:00,880
doesn't break through, the label still

431
00:15:58,720 --> 00:16:02,560
owns the masters, still earns from

432
00:16:00,880 --> 00:16:04,880
whatever streams those recordings

433
00:16:02,560 --> 00:16:07,199
generate in perpetuity, and the sunk

434
00:16:04,880 --> 00:16:09,600
cost of the advance is just the price of

435
00:16:07,199 --> 00:16:12,399
doing business. It's the same logic a

436
00:16:09,600 --> 00:16:14,480
venture capital firm uses. fund 10 bets,

437
00:16:12,399 --> 00:16:16,560
expect seven to lose money, and make it

438
00:16:14,480 --> 00:16:18,560
all back on the three that hit. And the

439
00:16:16,560 --> 00:16:20,320
scale of these companies goes way beyond

440
00:16:18,560 --> 00:16:23,680
what you'd expect. The three major

441
00:16:20,320 --> 00:16:26,639
labels, Universal, Sony, and Warner,

442
00:16:23,680 --> 00:16:28,720
collectively control roughly 65 to 70%

443
00:16:26,639 --> 00:16:30,639
of the entire global recorded music

444
00:16:28,720 --> 00:16:35,279
industry. That is an industry that

445
00:16:30,639 --> 00:16:38,560
generated 31.7 billion in 2025.

446
00:16:35,279 --> 00:16:41,360
Universal alone posted 14.4 billion in

447
00:16:38,560 --> 00:16:43,600
total revenue that year. Sony's combined

448
00:16:41,360 --> 00:16:46,240
recorded music and publishing revenue

449
00:16:43,600 --> 00:16:47,920
topped $3 billion in a single quarter

450
00:16:46,240 --> 00:16:51,279
for the first time in the last quarter

451
00:16:47,920 --> 00:16:53,440
of 2025. These are not boutique creative

452
00:16:51,279 --> 00:16:55,440
shops. These are global corporations

453
00:16:53,440 --> 00:16:57,279
with market capitalizations in the tens

454
00:16:55,440 --> 00:16:59,680
of billions. And the asset on their

455
00:16:57,279 --> 00:17:01,920
balance sheet is a library of songs.

456
00:16:59,680 --> 00:17:04,079
Every time you stream a song, play music

457
00:17:01,920 --> 00:17:06,160
at a wedding, hear a track in a movie

458
00:17:04,079 --> 00:17:08,160
trailer, or walk into a store where

459
00:17:06,160 --> 00:17:10,959
something is playing over the speakers,

460
00:17:08,160 --> 00:17:13,039
the odds are roughly 7 in 10 that the

461
00:17:10,959 --> 00:17:16,480
payment for that usage flows back to one

462
00:17:13,039 --> 00:17:18,720
of three companies. Three companies, 70%

463
00:17:16,480 --> 00:17:20,400
of that entire market, and the bulk of

464
00:17:18,720 --> 00:17:22,319
what they're selling is the right to use

465
00:17:20,400 --> 00:17:24,240
music they recorded years or decades

466
00:17:22,319 --> 00:17:26,079
ago. But here's the part that even

467
00:17:24,240 --> 00:17:28,720
people inside the music industry don't

468
00:17:26,079 --> 00:17:30,720
fully appreciate. If a music catalog is

469
00:17:28,720 --> 00:17:33,200
a financial asset that appreciates over

470
00:17:30,720 --> 00:17:35,200
time, pays a steady income stream, and

471
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:37,440
can be modeled and forecasted the way

472
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:39,520
you'd model a bond or a piece of real

473
00:17:37,440 --> 00:17:41,840
estate, then it was only a matter of

474
00:17:39,520 --> 00:17:44,480
time before Wall Street noticed. And

475
00:17:41,840 --> 00:17:47,039
Wall Street didn't just notice. Wall

476
00:17:44,480 --> 00:17:49,120
Street showed up in 1997 with an idea

477
00:17:47,039 --> 00:17:51,440
that turned songs into something nobody

478
00:17:49,120 --> 00:17:52,960
had ever imagined they could become.

479
00:17:51,440 --> 00:17:55,280
There's another layer to this that

480
00:17:52,960 --> 00:17:57,520
hardly ever comes up, and it has to do

481
00:17:55,280 --> 00:17:59,840
with who controls the pipes. The major

482
00:17:57,520 --> 00:18:01,840
labels don't just own music. They also

483
00:17:59,840 --> 00:18:03,360
control distribution. If you're an

484
00:18:01,840 --> 00:18:05,679
independent artist trying to get your

485
00:18:03,360 --> 00:18:08,799
music onto Spotify or Apple Music, you

486
00:18:05,679 --> 00:18:10,720
can use a service like Drokid or Touncor

487
00:18:08,799 --> 00:18:13,520
and those services will put your songs

488
00:18:10,720 --> 00:18:16,160
on the platforms for a flat fee or a

489
00:18:13,520 --> 00:18:19,280
small commission. But the majors don't

490
00:18:16,160 --> 00:18:21,919
need those services. Universal, Sony,

491
00:18:19,280 --> 00:18:23,760
and Warner each run their own massive

492
00:18:21,919 --> 00:18:25,440
distribution arms that handle the

493
00:18:23,760 --> 00:18:28,160
logistics of getting music from the

494
00:18:25,440 --> 00:18:29,919
label to the listener at a global scale.

495
00:18:28,160 --> 00:18:31,919
And those distribution arms don't just

496
00:18:29,919 --> 00:18:33,840
serve their own rosters. They also

497
00:18:31,919 --> 00:18:35,440
distribute music for independent labels

498
00:18:33,840 --> 00:18:37,360
and artists who want the reach and

499
00:18:35,440 --> 00:18:39,600
infrastructure that only a major can

500
00:18:37,360 --> 00:18:41,679
provide in exchange for a piece of the

501
00:18:39,600 --> 00:18:43,360
revenue. So, the label isn't just the

502
00:18:41,679 --> 00:18:45,440
landlord who owns the building. The

503
00:18:43,360 --> 00:18:47,600
label also owns the road that leads to

504
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:49,600
the building. If you want your music in

505
00:18:47,600 --> 00:18:51,679
front of the largest possible audience,

506
00:18:49,600 --> 00:18:53,280
the most efficient path runs through one

507
00:18:51,679 --> 00:18:55,120
of the three companies that already

508
00:18:53,280 --> 00:18:56,960
dominate the market. Now, think about

509
00:18:55,120 --> 00:18:59,039
the value of the assets these companies

510
00:18:56,960 --> 00:19:01,360
are sitting on. And think about how that

511
00:18:59,039 --> 00:19:02,880
value behaves over time. This is one of

512
00:19:01,360 --> 00:19:04,400
the strangest things about the music

513
00:19:02,880 --> 00:19:06,640
business, and it's the opposite of

514
00:19:04,400 --> 00:19:08,559
almost every other industry. Most

515
00:19:06,640 --> 00:19:10,799
consumer products lose value the moment

516
00:19:08,559 --> 00:19:13,039
they're sold. A new car is worth less

517
00:19:10,799 --> 00:19:14,799
the day you drive it off the lot. A new

518
00:19:13,039 --> 00:19:18,000
phone is worth less the week after the

519
00:19:14,799 --> 00:19:21,039
next model drops. Music is the opposite.

520
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:23,760
Old music, music that was recorded 20,

521
00:19:21,039 --> 00:19:25,600
30, 50 years ago, is more valuable today

522
00:19:23,760 --> 00:19:27,760
than it has been at almost any point

523
00:19:25,600 --> 00:19:30,720
since it was released. Streaming did

524
00:19:27,760 --> 00:19:32,640
that. Before streaming, a 1975 album

525
00:19:30,720 --> 00:19:34,880
would eventually go out of print. The

526
00:19:32,640 --> 00:19:37,120
CDs would stop being manufactured. The

527
00:19:34,880 --> 00:19:39,039
revenue would taper off. But streaming

528
00:19:37,120 --> 00:19:41,200
made every song ever recorded

529
00:19:39,039 --> 00:19:43,679
permanently available. A track from the

530
00:19:41,200 --> 00:19:45,280
1970s now sits on Spotify right next to

531
00:19:43,679 --> 00:19:47,120
this week's number one, earning

532
00:19:45,280 --> 00:19:49,679
fractions of a cent per play

533
00:19:47,120 --> 00:19:52,320
indefinitely across every country with

534
00:19:49,679 --> 00:19:55,440
an internet connection. And that means

535
00:19:52,320 --> 00:19:58,880
the value of what the labels own keeps

536
00:19:55,440 --> 00:20:02,240
going up year after year without any new

537
00:19:58,880 --> 00:20:04,400
investment. That is why UMG's 2021 IPO

538
00:20:02,240 --> 00:20:06,000
priced the way it did. Vivventi, the

539
00:20:04,400 --> 00:20:09,360
French conglomerate that had controlled

540
00:20:06,000 --> 00:20:11,120
Universal for 20 years, spun off 60% of

541
00:20:09,360 --> 00:20:13,039
UMG's shares to its existing

542
